::: {.doc-subject}

Target the footnotes section of the footer.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_locations.py#L907-L976){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.CLASS}
```python
loc.footnotes()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

With `loc.footnotes()`, we can target only the footnotes in the table's footer. This is useful
when applying custom styling with the [`tab_style()`](`great_tables.GT.tab_style`) method. That
method has a `locations=` argument and this class should be used there to perform the targeting.
The 'footnotes' location is generated by
[`tab_footnote()`](`great_tables.GT.tab_footnote`). To target both the footnotes and source
notes sections together, use [`loc.footer()`](`great_tables.loc.footer`) instead.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [LocFootnotes]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A `LocFootnotes` object, which is used for a `locations=` argument if specifying the
    footnotes section.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Let's use a subset of the `gtcars` dataset in a new table. We'll add a footnote (with
[`tab_footnote()`](`great_tables.GT.tab_footnote`)) and a source note, then style only the
footnotes section inside [`tab_style()`](`great_tables.GT.tab_style`) with
`locations=loc.footnotes()`.

::: {#6200e5f3 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, style, loc
from great_tables.data import gtcars

gtcars_mini = gtcars[["mfr", "model", "msrp"]].head(5)

(
    GT(gtcars_mini)
    .tab_footnote(
        footnote="Prices in USD.",
        locations=loc.column_labels(columns="msrp")
    )
    .tab_source_note(source_note="From edmunds.com")
    .tab_style(
        style=style.fill(color="#FFFFDD"),
        locations=loc.footnotes()
    )
)
```

